<article class="env-proposition" id="65C4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/65C4">Proposition <span data-tag="65C4">6.2.3.15</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be a semitopos, and suppose given a pullback square </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { X' \ar [r]^{g'} \ar [d]^{f'} &  X \ar [d]^{f} \\ S' \ar [r]^{g} &  S } \]
</div>
  <p> in $\calX $. If $f$ is an effective epimorphism, then so is $f'$. The converse holds if $g$ is an effective epimorphism. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $g^{\ast }: \calX ^{/S} \rightarrow \calX ^{/S'}$ be a pullback functor. Without loss of generality we may suppose that $f' = g^{\ast } f$. Let $U_{\bullet }: \Nerve (\cDelta _{+})^{op} \rightarrow \calX $ be a Čech nerve of $f$. Since $g^{\ast }$ is left exact (being a right adjoint), we conclude that $g^{\ast } \circ U_{\bullet }$ is a Čech nerve of $f'$. If $f$ is an effective epimorphism, then $U_{\bullet }$ is a colimit diagram. Because colimits in $\calX $ are universal, $g^{\ast } \circ U_{\bullet }$ is also a colimit diagram, so that $f'$ is an effective epimorphism. 
    <p>
      Conversely, suppose that $f'$ and $g$ are effective epimorphisms. Corollary <a href="/tag/80A3" data-tag="80A3">6.2.3.12</a> implies that $g \circ f'$ is an effective epimorphism. The commutativity of the diagram implies that $f \circ g'$ is an effective epimorphism, so that $f$ is an effective epimorphism (Corollary <a href="/tag/80A3" data-tag="80A3">6.2.3.12</a> again). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>